# Publish a release as a container image

A container image is this service packed with everything it needs to run.
GitHub's container registry (GHCR, at `ghcr.io`) is a shelf for images:
once a version is there, a server or a hosting service can fetch and run
exactly that version, for example `docker pull {{ image }}:0.2.0`. This page
is the steps, in order, for every release.

How it works, in one paragraph: you push a version tag, such as `v0.2.0`.
The workflow `.github/workflows/release.yml` then checks the tag, builds the
image, runs it once the way the `image` check does, pushes it to
`{{ image }}:0.2.0`, fetches it back and runs it again, and makes a release
page on GitHub. No password or token is stored anywhere: the push uses the
run's own token, which GitHub makes for that run and which stops working
when it ends.

Nothing has to be set up before the first tag. The first push creates the
package (GitHub's name for the image and its versions) under the account
that owns this repository.

## 1. Publish

1. Set the version. The version is written in `pyproject.toml`
   (`version = "0.1.0"` to start). For a new release, ask the agent to raise
   it in a pull request, for example "raise the version to 0.2.0 with
   `uv version 0.2.0`", and add what changed to `CHANGELOG.md`. Merge it when
   its checks are green.
2. Push the tag. The tag is the version with a `v` in front, on the merged
   `main`. In a terminal, in this repository:

   ```bash
   git fetch origin
   git tag v0.2.0 origin/main
   git push origin v0.2.0
   ```

   You push the tag yourself, not the agent: letting a version out is a
   person's decision.
3. Open the repository's **Actions** tab and the run named **Release**. When
   it is green, its summary names the image and the command to fetch it, and
   a release page is under **Releases** on GitHub.

## 2. Who can fetch the image

The image is linked to this repository and, on its first push, takes the
repository's visibility: from a public repository anyone can fetch it, from
a private one only people who can read the repository. (GitHub's
documentation says a new package starts private; a trial on 2026-10-29 got
a public package from a public repository.) Check it once: open the
repository's main page, **Packages** in the right-hand column, and read
whether the package says **Public** or **Private**.

- **To fetch a private image** from your own machine, log in once with a
  personal access token (classic) that has only the `read:packages` scope:
  `docker login ghcr.io --username <your GitHub account>`, and paste the
  token when it asks for a password. Never paste the token into the chat.
- **To let anyone fetch a private repository's image:** on the package's
  page, **Package settings** > **Danger Zone** > **Change visibility** >
  **Public**. Anyone can then read the code inside the image. This cannot be
  undone: a public package never becomes private again.

## 3. What a private image costs to keep

A public image is free to keep and to fetch. A private one counts against
what the account's plan includes. GitHub's figures, read 2026-10-29
(<https://docs.github.com/en/billing/concepts/product-billing/github-packages>):

| Plan | Storage included | Fetching included, per month |
|---|---|---|
| GitHub Free (a personal account or an organization) | 500 MB | 1 GB |
| GitHub Pro, GitHub Team | 2 GB | 10 GB |

- The storage is shared with the files GitHub Actions runs keep (artifacts).
- Every version stays until you delete it, so each release adds its whole
  size. This project's image as the template makes it is about 45 MB (a
  trial on 2026-10-29) and grows with each dependency: on GitHub Free, about
  ten such versions fill the 500 MB.
- Fetching from a workflow in this repository does not count; fetching
  from your machine or a server does.
- The same page says the container registry is *currently* free of charge
  for storage and fetching, and that GitHub will say so at least a month
  before that changes. Plan for the limits above anyway.
- Past what is included: with no payment method on the account, GitHub
  blocks further use; with one, it bills the extra, up to any budget set in
  the account's billing settings. GitHub emails at 90% and 100% of what is
  included.

To delete an old version: on the package's page, under "Recent Versions",
**View and manage all versions**, then the **⋯** next to a version >
**Delete version**.

## 4. The signed record of how it was built

On a **public** repository, each image gets a signed record (an
*attestation*) saying which workflow, in which repository, built it from
which commit. Anyone can check it:

```bash
gh attestation verify oci://{{ image }}:0.2.0 --repo {{ owner if owner else '<your GitHub account>' }}/{{ project_name }}
```

On a **private** repository, GitHub makes these records only on its
GitHub Enterprise Cloud plan. On any other plan the run publishes the image
without one, does not fail, and says so in its summary: "Published without a
signed record".

## When a release fails

Nothing is pushed until the checks before it have passed. The run says
which step stopped.

- **"The tag and the version differ."** The tag says one version,
  `pyproject.toml` another. Decide which one is right. If the file is right,
  delete the tag and push the right one. If the tag is right, change the
  version in a pull request first, merge it, then delete the tag and push it
  again. To delete a tag: `git push origin --delete v0.2.0`, then
  `git tag -d v0.2.0`.
- **"The tag is not on main."** The tag points at a commit that never went
  through a pull request. Delete it and tag `origin/main` as in step 1.
- **"Run the image and read its first log line" failed.** The image builds
  but does not start. The `image` check on pull requests runs the same
  check, so the cause is usually something that changed outside one, such as
  a base image. Ask the agent to run `docker build` and `docker run` on it.
- **The push says `denied` or `permission_denied`.** A package with this
  name already exists under the account and is not linked to this
  repository. On the package's page, **Package settings** > **Manage Actions
  access** > **Add repository**, choose this repository with the **Write**
  role, then delete the tag and push it again.

Pushing the same tag again replaces the image under that version; the
earlier one stays in the package, without a version, until you delete it.
Deleting a tag turns its release page into a draft, so after pushing it
again, delete the draft under **Releases**.
